  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Dotcoms to watch: Addressing the scholarly publishing industry\u2019s challenges through technology (2024) in Information Services & Use.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Dotcoms to watch: Addressing the scholarly publishing industry\u2019s challenges through technology (2024) in Information Services & Use is authored by Martijn Roelandse, Adam Day and Tijmen Altena. Topics: Publishing, Scholarly communication, Library science, Business and World Wide Web.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">No citations yet.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Limited online visibility beyond citations.</p>",
  "provenance": {},
  "story_type": "ArticleStory",
  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2024 article is a conference-linked scholarly contribution on technology responses to problems in scholarly publishing, with immediate relevance to research integrity, peer review, and publishing workflows. The record shows early-stage visibility rather than demonstrated citation impact: it is open access, indexed in multiple repositories, and associated with a small but concrete evidence base for discovery and reuse.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">\u201cDotcoms to watch: Addressing the scholarly publishing industry\u2019s challenges through technology\u201d reports on the Dotcoms to Watch session at APE 2024 and expands the discussion through contributions from five of the selected startups. The article is situated at the intersection of publishing, scholarly communication, and technology, with concepts extending into library science, business, computer science, and law. That mix reflects the paper\u2019s practical orientation: it is less a theoretical study than a snapshot of tools and services being developed to address live industry problems.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The paper\u2019s central contribution is topical rather than citation-based at this stage. Its abstract indicates attention to research integrity, papermill detection, and peer reviewer identification, placing the work in an area of clear operational importance for publishers and related service providers. With zero citations recorded in OpenAlex so far, the impact story should be read as early and emerging, not absent; the available evidence simply shows that scholarly uptake has not yet accumulated.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">There are still meaningful signals of reach. The article is openly available under a diamond open-access model, and repository indexing shows multiple open instances plus a bibliographic record in DBLP. WorldCat holdings indicate library discovery, while the data-citation pipeline reports successful processing for cited datasets and dataset attribution. Together, these signals suggest the work is findable and reusable across research infrastructure, even though downstream citation evidence remains limited.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The authorship also reflects cross-sector engagement, bringing together contributors affiliated with a consultancy, a media company, a university, a hospital, an industry organization, and a technical university. That diversity fits the article\u2019s applied subject matter and supports its role as a bridge between scholarly publishing practice and technology development. Overall, the record portrays a timely, practice-oriented article with early dissemination and infrastructure visibility, but with little evidence yet of broader academic or public uptake.</p>",
  "narrative_source": "template"
}